        "id": 1097438
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-24T01:14:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could you please explain to me how this is different from OVERRIDE_OVERRIDABLE_COMPAT_CHANGES, and what function did CompatChange#getOverridable() have before this change. E.g. why are the overrides for non-overridable changes allowed at all?\n\nIf OVERRIDE_OVERRIDABLE_COMPAT_CHANGES is a stronger permission, doesn\u0027t the same protection level make them equivalent?",
      "revId": "0c80b67bdd483d7c47817e622cabc6b388170402",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c2e26eb7_be324b67",
        "filename": "core/res/AndroidManifest.xml",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 5125,
      "author": {
        "id": 1061116
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-24T14:51:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think it\u0027s easier to audit separate permissions, even if they have the same protection.\n\nThe new permission is explicitly for allowing overrides on release builds and non-test apps. We will only give it to Phonesky and Shell. The existing permission is given to Settings for example.\n\nOverridable is a concept that I added in https://r.android.com/c/tools/platform-compat/+/1540125/2/java/android/compat/annotation/Overridable.java, which explicitly denotes changeids that are overridable in release builds. Overrides are always allowed in non-release builds or apks marked as test-apks.",
      "parentUuid": "410b91fd_a1a8a6ea",
      "revId": "0c80b67bdd483d7c47817e622cabc6b388170402",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "26e38af6_a77edcff",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/compat/PlatformCompat.java",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 186,
      "author": {
        "id": 1097438
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-24T01:14:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since all setOverrides* methods here have the same visibility and access level, would it make sense to:\n1. Verify if only the overridable changes are requested everywhere.\n2. Verify if the caller that requested overrides on a user build has the right permission everywhere.\n3. Rename this method to setOverridesNoRelaunch.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 186,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 186,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "0c80b67bdd483d7c47817e622cabc6b388170402",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
